const markdown = "# SERVANA PRIVACY POLICY AND DATA PROTECTION NOTICE\n\n**Effective Date:** 01st January 2026\n**Last Updated:** 01st August 2026\n\n**Owner, Operator, Publisher, Contracting Entity, Data Controller and/or Data Processor, as applicable:**\n**Citrus Labs Limited**\n**P.O. Box 23983 - 00100, Nairobi, Kenya**\n**Email:** [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke)\n\n---\n\n## 1. SCOPE, APPLICATION, AND LEGAL EFFECT\n\n1.1. This Servana Privacy Policy and Data Protection Notice (“**Policy**” or “**Notice**”) governs the collection, recording, organisation, structuring, storage, adaptation, alteration, retrieval, consultation, access, use, disclosure, transmission, dissemination, alignment, combination, restriction, erasure, destruction, retention, safeguarding, audit, and other Processing of Personal Data by Citrus Labs Limited in connection with Servana, including the Servana website, web application, SaaS dashboard, merchant onboarding flow, merchant account portal, branch dashboard, admin console, subscription flow, account settings, customer support channels, APIs, emails, notifications, reports, exports, billing records, audit logs, and related services.\n\n1.2. This Policy is specifically addressed to the **Merchant Administrator**, also referred to in Servana as the **Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator**. The Merchant Administrator is the registering business owner, manager, authorised operator, subscriber, account owner, Customer representative, and primary merchant-side administrator who self-registers a merchant business account, causes the relevant merchant tenant to be created by the system, completes first-time setup, selects the merchant service fee tier, creates Merchant Branch accounts, adds initial Merchant Branch account user and Merchant Human Resource account user email addresses, manages the merchant profile, views merchant-level and branch-level reports, manages certain merchant staff lifecycle controls, receives daily branch reports, views platform fee records, and performs merchant-level administrative functions within the Customer’s own merchant tenant.\n\n1.3. This Policy is not drafted for Super Administrators, Merchant Branch account users, Merchant Human Resource account users, Merchant Finance account users, Merchant Front Office account users, Merchant Personnel account users, Merchant Audit account users, General End-User portal users, or service-recipient clients as primary addressees. Those actors may be subject to separate, additional, role-specific, or context-specific notices. Where this Policy refers to such actors, it does so because the Merchant Administrator may create, invite, view, administer, restrict, suspend, receive reports about, or otherwise interact with data relating to those actors within the Merchant Administrator’s own merchant tenant, subject to Servana’s role-based, tenant-based, branch-based, and permission-based access controls.\n\n1.4. By accessing, registering for, using, configuring, administering, managing, subscribing to, paying for, operating, or otherwise interacting with Servana as a Merchant Administrator, the Merchant Administrator acknowledges that Personal Data will be Processed as described in this Policy, subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, contractual terms, Customer responsibilities, Citrus Labs Limited’s data protection roles, and the privacy rights available to Data Subjects.\n\n1.5. This Policy is intended for publication and incorporation through Servana’s website, web application, SaaS dashboard, signup flow, merchant onboarding flow, subscription flow, account portal, help centre, legal page, customer onboarding materials, and any other lawful electronic or written publication channel used by Citrus Labs Limited.\n\n---\n\n## 2. RELATIONSHIP WITH OTHER SERVANA LEGAL DOCUMENTS\n\n2.1. This Policy forms part of the legal and operational framework governing Servana and must be read together with Servana’s Terms and Conditions, SaaS subscription terms, customer agreements, order forms, data processing agreements, acceptable use policies, security policies, support policies, billing terms, service notices, role-based access disclosures, and any written agreement between Citrus Labs Limited and the relevant Customer.\n\n2.2. This Policy does not replace any separate data processing agreement, customer agreement, employment agreement, contractor agreement, confidentiality agreement, acceptable use policy, or subscription agreement. Where a signed written agreement contains more specific provisions governing a particular Processing activity, that agreement shall apply to the relevant Processing activity to the extent of the conflict, unless Applicable Law requires otherwise.\n\n2.3. This Policy is a privacy and data protection notice. It is not, by itself, a complete commercial contract for subscription fees, payment terms, service levels, warranties, indemnities, liability caps, intellectual property ownership, acceptable use, service availability, or technical support, except where privacy-related provisions necessarily cross-reference those matters.\n\n---\n\n## 3. DEFINITIONS AND INTERPRETATION\n\n3.1. In this Policy, unless the context requires otherwise:\n\n3.1.1. “**Account**” means a Servana account, merchant account, tenant account, user account, administrator account, branch account, staff account, internal account, or authenticated profile created, invited, authorised, suspended, restricted, maintained, or deleted in Servana.\n\n3.1.2. “**Administrator**” means a person or role with administrative permissions in Servana, including, depending on context, a Merchant Administrator, Super Administrator, Merchant Branch account user, Merchant Human Resource account user, Merchant Finance account user, or any other authorised administrator.\n\n3.1.3. “**Affiliate**” means any entity that directly or indirectly controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with Citrus Labs Limited, where such relationship exists.\n\n3.1.4. “**Applicable Law**” means all privacy, data protection, cybersecurity, consumer protection, electronic communications, e-commerce, records, tax, accounting, employment, evidentiary, regulatory, court, law enforcement, and other laws, regulations, binding directives, lawful guidance, orders, or rules applicable to Citrus Labs Limited, Servana, a Customer, a Data Subject, a Processing activity, or a relevant jurisdiction, including, where applicable, Kenya’s Data Protection Act, 2019, applicable Kenyan data protection regulations, lawful guidance of Kenya’s Office of the Data Protection Commissioner, African Union data protection principles, and privacy or data protection laws in other African jurisdictions where Servana may operate or where relevant Data Subjects are protected.\n\n3.1.5. “**Citrus Labs Limited**”, “**Citrus**”, “**we**”, “**us**”, or “**our**” means Citrus Labs Limited, P.O. Box 23983 - 00100, Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n3.1.6. “**Client**” means a service recipient recorded in Servana by or on behalf of a merchant, including a person who receives or is scheduled to receive services from a Customer’s branch, such as a barbershop, salon, spa, massage parlour, grooming studio, beauty parlour, or similar service-based SME.\n\n3.1.7. “**Controller**” or “**Data Controller**” means the natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which, alone or jointly with others, determines the purposes and means of Processing Personal Data.\n\n3.1.8. “**Customer**” means the merchant business, organisation, subscriber, account owner, business account, or other legal or commercial entity that registers for, subscribes to, configures, receives, or uses Servana for business operations.\n\n3.1.9. “**Customer Data**” means data, records, files, content, configurations, communications, instructions, Personal Data, business data, operational data, client records, staff records, branch records, service records, queue records, appointment records, service session records, invoices, receipts, offline payment records, platform fee records, commission records, reports, exports, audit logs, support records, and other information submitted to, generated in, stored in, or Processed through Servana by or on behalf of a Customer.\n\n3.1.10. “**Data Processor**” or “**Processor**” means a person or entity that Processes Personal Data on behalf of a Data Controller.\n\n3.1.11. “**Data Subject**” means an identified or identifiable natural person to whom Personal Data relates.\n\n3.1.12. “**Device Data**” means information relating to a browser, device, network, session, or technical environment, including IP address, user agent, browser type, operating system, device type, language setting, timezone, approximate location derived from IP address, session identifiers, cookie identifiers, diagnostic data, and related technical metadata.\n\n3.1.13. “**Merchant Administrator**” or “**Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator**” means the Customer-side business owner, manager, authorised operator, subscriber, account owner, or primary merchant-side administrator who self-registers the merchant business account, after which the system creates the merchant tenant and assigns the registering user as Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator.\n\n3.1.14. “**Merchant Branch**” means a physical or operational business location created by a Merchant Administrator within a merchant tenant, including, by way of example, a Westlands branch, Kilimani salon branch, CBD barbershop branch, hotel spa branch, massage parlour branch, grooming studio branch, or similar operating unit.\n\n3.1.15. “**Personal Data**” or “**Personal Information**” means any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person, whether directly or indirectly, and includes any equivalent concept under Applicable Law.\n\n3.1.16. “**Platform**”, “**Service**”, “**Servana**”, or “**Servana by Citrus**” means the multi-tenant SaaS web platform owned and operated by Citrus Labs Limited for service-based SMEs to manage merchant onboarding, merchant branches, staff access, client records, walk-ins, appointments, queues, service sessions, invoices, offline payment records, receipts, personnel commissions, platform fees, reports, notifications, audit logs, and related operational workflows.\n\n3.1.17. “**Processing**”, “**Process**”, or “**Processed**” means any operation or set of operations performed on Personal Data or on sets of Personal Data, whether or not by automated means.\n\n3.1.18. “**Sensitive Personal Data**” means Personal Data subject to heightened protection under Applicable Law, including, where applicable, health data, biometric data, genetic data, children’s data, precise location data, financial information requiring special protection, racial or ethnic origin, religious or philosophical beliefs, political opinions, trade union membership, sex life, sexual orientation, or any other category treated as sensitive, special-category, restricted, or protected under Applicable Law.\n\n3.1.19. “**Subprocessor**” means any third party appointed by Citrus Labs Limited to Process Personal Data on behalf of Citrus Labs Limited where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor.\n\n3.1.20. “**Super Administrator**” means the Citrus Labs Limited platform-owner role that governs Servana at platform level, including platform-wide settings, service fee rules, billing cycles, preferred-personnel waiting fees, platform reports, platform fee ledgers, suspicious usage monitoring, internal Citrus platform roles, feature flags, merchant suspension, merchant deactivation, billing enforcement, abuse response, and platform-policy enforcement.\n\n3.1.21. “**Usage Data**” means information relating to use of Servana, including pages viewed, dashboards accessed, features used, clicks, timestamps, login events, configuration changes, branch creation activity, staff invitation activity, report access, export activity, support activity, API requests, session duration, error events, audit events, and operational telemetry.\n\n3.1.22. “**User**” means any individual who accesses or uses Servana, including a Merchant Administrator, Super Administrator, Merchant Branch account user, Merchant Human Resource account user, Merchant Finance account user, Merchant Front Office account user, Merchant Personnel account user, Merchant Audit account user, invited user, support user, or other authorised account user.\n\n3.2. References to “including” mean “including without limitation”. References to “law” include statutes, regulations, binding guidance, lawful orders, and competent authority decisions. Headings are for convenience only and do not limit interpretation. Singular terms include plural terms and plural terms include singular terms where context permits.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DESCRIPTION OF SERVANA\n\n4.1. Servana is a multi-tenant SaaS web platform for service-based SMEs, including barbershops, salons, massage parlours, spas, grooming studios, beauty parlours, and similar appointment-based or walk-in businesses.\n\n4.2. Servana is designed to operate as a service-operations platform through which merchant businesses can digitise, control, and audit day-to-day operations, including merchant onboarding, merchant branches, staff and personnel access, front-office operations, client records, walk-ins, appointments, queue management, service delivery tracking, client choice of next available personnel or preferred personnel, invoice generation, offline payment recording, receipt generation, personnel commission visibility, client data protection, contact access control, platform fee calculation, service fee tier invoice pricing, merchant financial visibility, and audit-ready operational and financial records.\n\n4.3. Servana must not be treated solely as a booking application, point-of-sale system, payment processor, invoicing tool, staff roster, or reporting tool. It is a service-operations SaaS platform intended to help Customers run clients, staff, services, queues, invoices, commissions, platform fees, reports, and audit records from one secure web dashboard.\n\n4.4. Servana is structured around tenant isolation. Each merchant operates as an isolated tenant. A merchant’s data must not be accessible, inferable, editable, exportable, or enumerable by another merchant, subject only to controlled Citrus Labs Limited platform-level workflows, lawful support access, security operations, legal compliance, billing enforcement, abuse controls, and other lawful exceptions.\n\n4.5. Servana records offline or off-platform payments. Servana may record payment method, amount, reference, status, validation details, payment notes, partial payment records, split payment records, external refund records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, disputes, receipts, and related audit trails, but Servana does not, by this Policy alone, represent that it directly processes client-to-merchant payments inside the platform.\n\n---\n\n## 5. MERCHANT ADMINISTRATOR ROLE AND PRIVACY TREATMENT\n\n5.1. The Merchant Administrator is the primary Customer-side account owner and merchant tenant administrator addressed by this Policy.\n\n5.2. The Merchant Administrator self-registers the merchant business account. After self-registration, Servana creates the merchant tenant and assigns the registering user as Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator. Super Administrator manual approval of Merchant Administrator self-registration is not required for dashboard access, and Servana does not require merchant KYC submission, compliance document submission, or activation document submission as part of the Merchant Administrator self-registration workflow unless a separate lawful feature, agreement, or regulatory requirement is later enabled.\n\n5.3. After account creation, the Merchant Administrator must complete first-time setup before accessing the Merchant Administrator dashboard. First-time setup may include selecting the merchant service fee tier, completing and managing the merchant profile, creating at least one Merchant Branch account, completing the Merchant Branch profile, adding initial Merchant Branch account user email addresses, adding initial Merchant Human Resource account user email addresses, selecting the relevant Merchant Branch of operation for each initial staff email address, triggering Magic Link welcome emails for invited users, and being redirected to the Merchant Administrator dashboard after setup completion.\n\n5.4. The Merchant Administrator may view all Merchant Branch accounts created under that Merchant Administrator, view merchant staff under those branches, view merchant-level revenue reports in real time across branches, view branch revenue performance for today, this week, last month, and the last three months, view branch services and pricing, view service revenue performance, view staff performance cumulatively per branch and per individual staff, view merchant-level platform fee records, receive daily branch day-close reports by email in PDF format, receive daily branch cash-up and reconciliation reports by email in PDF format, lock financial periods where required and permitted, and suspend inactive or abusive merchant users while preserving historical records and audit logs.\n\n5.5. The Merchant Administrator may add only Merchant Branch account user email addresses and Merchant Human Resource account user email addresses during the Merchant Administrator-controlled staff setup flow. The Merchant Administrator must not create or approve other account users outside the permitted flow, link Merchant Personnel to accounts, configure services or service pricing at Merchant Administrator level, configure branch personnel assignments, configure Merchant Personnel commissions directly, control whether Front Office users can record payments or issue receipts, or assign personnel to branches. Those actions are controlled by other Servana roles, permissions, or workflows.\n\n5.6. The Merchant Administrator may be treated as a representative of the Customer, an account owner, a Customer administrator, and, for Customer-controlled Personal Data, a person acting under the authority of the Customer. The Customer may be the Data Controller for many categories of Personal Data Processed in Servana, including client data, staff data, service records, appointments, invoices, offline payment records, receipts, commission records, and branch records, except where Citrus Labs Limited independently determines the relevant Processing purposes and means.\n\n---\n\n## 6. CITRUS LABS LIMITED’S DATA PROTECTION ROLES\n\n6.1. Citrus Labs Limited may act as an independent Data Controller where it determines the purposes and means of Processing, including Processing relating to merchant account registration, Citrus account administration, subscription administration, platform fee records, product telemetry, security monitoring, fraud prevention, abuse controls, platform-policy enforcement, billing enforcement, support operations, analytics, service improvement, direct communications, legal compliance, regulatory cooperation, legal claims, and Citrus business administration.\n\n6.2. Citrus Labs Limited may act as a Data Processor where it Processes Customer Data on behalf of a Customer that determines the relevant purposes and means of Processing, including client records, merchant staff records, HR records, branch records, appointment records, walk-in records, queue records, service session records, invoices, receipts, offline payment records, payment validation records, commission records, disputes, external refund records, reports, and Customer-configured workflows.\n\n6.3. Citrus Labs Limited may act as a service provider, operator, SaaS platform provider, joint controller, independent controller, processor, subprocessor appointor, or other legally recognised role depending on the specific Processing activity, Applicable Law, contractual arrangements, and factual context.\n\n6.4. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor, Citrus Labs Limited Processes Personal Data on documented Customer instructions, subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, security obligations, support requirements, data processing agreements, and the need to preserve platform integrity.\n\n6.5. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Controller, Citrus Labs Limited may determine lawful purposes, lawful bases, retention periods, security measures, disclosures, analytics, compliance responses, and enforcement actions in accordance with this Policy and Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 7. CUSTOMER-CONTROLLED DATA AND CITRUS-CONTROLLED DATA\n\n7.1. Customer-controlled data may include merchant profile data, business category data, merchant logo data, branch profile data, branch addresses, branch contact details, branch operating calendars, branch status records, staff invitation records, staff profile data, HR records, employment status, personnel service eligibility, availability calendars, client profiles, client contact details, visit history, service history, appointment history, queue history, assigned personnel history, preferred personnel history, service notes, invoices, receipt records, payment records, payment references, split payment records, partial payment records, external refund records, disputes, commission rules, commission liabilities, cash-up records, reconciliation records, consent records, communication preferences, reports, exports, and Customer audit logs.\n\n7.2. Citrus-controlled data may include Merchant Administrator registration metadata, account metadata, authentication logs, Magic Link token metadata, platform fee settings, subscription and billing administration records, service fee tier configuration records, platform usage telemetry, error logs, security logs, abuse monitoring records, suspicious activity records, support records, Citrus legal records, product analytics, system performance data, and internal Citrus operational records.\n\n7.3. Certain data may be mixed-context data. For example, an audit log may contain a Merchant Administrator actor identifier, merchant tenant identifier, branch identifier, action, target entity, old values, new values, severity, event status, IP address, user agent, record hash, previous record hash, and timestamp. Citrus Labs Limited may control the audit system for platform integrity, while the underlying client, staff, branch, invoice, payment, or service record may be Customer-controlled data.\n\n7.4. Citrus Labs Limited may retain rights in non-personal platform metadata, aggregated information, anonymised information, de-identified information, product telemetry, security insights, service-improvement insights, and statistical data, provided such Processing is conducted in accordance with Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 8. CATEGORIES OF PERSONAL DATA PROCESSED\n\n8.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process the following Personal Data relating to the Merchant Administrator:\n\n8.1.1. **Identity Data**, including name, display name, role, account identifier, merchant owner status, merchant administrator status, internal user identifier, and profile information.\n\n8.1.2. **Contact Data**, including email address, phone number, business contact details, support contact details, notification address, and communication identifiers.\n\n8.1.3. **Authentication Data**, including Magic Link token metadata, token issue time, token expiry time, token status, email verification status, login timestamps, failed login attempts, account status, role status, suspension status, session identifiers, rate-limit events, optional MFA status where enabled, and security challenge metadata.\n\n8.1.4. **Merchant and Organisation Data**, including business name, business category, merchant profile, merchant logo, merchant tenant identifier, branch identifiers, branch creation records, branch profile data, service fee tier selection, merchant status, platform fee status, billing cycle data, overdue status, subscription data, and merchant-facing statements.\n\n8.1.5. **Role and Permission Data**, including Merchant Administrator privileges, staff invitation permissions, branch creation permissions, report visibility, financial period lock permissions where applicable, staff activation, suspension, removal, deletion, or restriction permissions where applicable, and role history.\n\n8.1.6. **Transaction, Subscription, and Billing-Related Data**, including account-opening fee records where applicable, platform fee ledger records, platform fee payment status, merchant balance records, branch-level platform fee debt tracking, service fee tier records, overdue balance records, suspension triggers, fee exemption records, platform fee disputes, billing audit logs, and merchant-facing platform fee statements.\n\n8.1.7. **Support and Communication Data**, including support requests, messages, attachments, screenshots, diagnostics, troubleshooting notes, feedback, complaints, administrative communications, and service correspondence.\n\n8.1.8. **Usage Data**, including dashboard access, branch creation activity, profile updates, staff invitation activity, report views, export requests, daily report access, platform fee ledger access, financial period lock activity, staff lifecycle actions, service performance report access, branch performance report access, and account configuration activity.\n\n8.1.9. **Device, Log, and Technical Data**, including IP address, user agent, browser type, device type, operating system, timezone, approximate location derived from IP address, API metadata, session logs, error logs, diagnostic logs, and performance metadata.\n\n8.1.10. **Security and Audit Data**, including actor identifiers, actor role, merchant identifier, branch identifier, action, target entity type, target entity identifier, old values, new values, severity, event status, IP address, user agent, record hash, previous record hash, unauthorised access attempts, duplicate prevention events, suspicious activity flags, and timestamps.\n\n8.1.11. **Preference Data**, including notification preferences, dashboard preferences, theme preferences, communication preferences, and settings where enabled.\n\n8.1.12. **Marketing, Relationship, and Feedback Data**, including lawful marketing preferences, survey responses, feedback, business relationship notes, product interest records, and opt-out records.\n\n8.2. Citrus Labs Limited may Process the following Personal Data relating to other actors within the Merchant Administrator’s tenant:\n\n8.2.1. **Merchant Branch account user data**, including identity data, email address, branch assignment, branch profile activity, operating calendar changes, service configuration activity, service pricing activity, queue actions, appointment actions, branch day opening and closing records, cash-up submission records, branch reports, and audit logs.\n\n8.2.2. **Merchant Human Resource account user data**, including staff invitation activity, staff profile creation, staff profile editing, role assignment activity, service eligibility assignments, availability calendar changes, employment data, staff roster exports, permission previews, suspension and deactivation actions, and staff access lifecycle records.\n\n8.2.3. **Merchant Finance account user data**, including invoice views, offline payment validation activity, payment reference records, receipt generation activity, dispute records, external refund records, cash-up review activity, commission liability review activity, platform fee visibility records, finance exports, and finance audit activity.\n\n8.2.4. **Merchant Front Office account user data**, including client registration activity, existing client retrieval activity, walk-in creation, appointment creation, appointment rescheduling, no-show marking, check-in records, queue action records, service session records, invoice creation, offline payment recording, validation submission records, notification activity, and speed-sensitive search records.\n\n8.2.5. **Merchant Personnel account user data**, including service provider identity, role, assigned branches, service eligibility, availability states, own assignments, own queue, own appointments, own service history, commission records, preferred-personnel requests, personally served client visibility records, and contact access attempt records.\n\n8.2.6. **Merchant Audit account user data**, including read-only audit activity, flagged items, high-risk event reviews, role change reviews, branch change reviews, invoice history views, payment validation log views, receipt log views, queue reassignment log views, preferred-personnel fee log views, contact-access or export-attempt reviews, and audit report export records where permitted.\n\n8.2.7. **Client record data**, including client profile, phone number, optional email, optional gender, visit history, service history, assigned personnel history, preferred personnel history, invoice history, receipt history, appointment history, queue participation, payment history, client preferences, consent records, communication preferences, notes, duplicate-client prevention records, and optional future portal records where enabled.\n\n---\n\n## 9. SENSITIVE PERSONAL DATA AND SPECIAL-CATEGORY DATA\n\n9.1. Servana does not require Merchant Administrators to submit Sensitive Personal Data for ordinary merchant account registration or routine merchant administration.\n\n9.2. Sensitive Personal Data may nevertheless be Processed where the Customer, Merchant Administrator, staff user, client, support requester, or other authorised person submits it into Servana, including through client notes, staff records, employment records, dispute evidence, refund evidence, service notes, support attachments, screenshots, communication records, consent records, or other Customer-controlled fields.\n\n9.3. The Customer and Merchant Administrator must not submit or cause the submission of Sensitive Personal Data unless there is a lawful basis, appropriate notice, required consent or authorisation where applicable, appropriate role-based access configuration, and compliance with Applicable Law.\n\n9.4. Where Citrus Labs Limited Processes Sensitive Personal Data, it will do so subject to Applicable Law, lawful basis requirements, access restrictions, retention controls, security safeguards, contractual obligations, and lawful Customer instructions where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor.\n\n---\n\n## 10. CHILDREN’S AND MINORS’ DATA\n\n10.1. Servana is intended for business use by merchants and authorised business users. It is not designed for independent use by children.\n\n10.2. Customers may record minors as service-recipient Clients where lawful and relevant to the Customer’s business operations. Such records may include client profile details, appointment history, service history, invoice history, receipt history, consent records, communication preferences, or guardian-related information where lawfully submitted.\n\n10.3. The Customer and Merchant Administrator are responsible for obtaining parental or guardian consent where required, issuing appropriate notices, limiting access, ensuring lawful Processing, and complying with child-protection and data protection requirements applicable to minors’ data.\n\n10.4. Citrus Labs Limited may restrict, delete, preserve, or refer minors’ data to the relevant Customer where required or permitted by Applicable Law, contractual obligations, or lawful Customer instructions.\n\n---\n\n## 11. SOURCES OF PERSONAL DATA\n\n11.1. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data directly from the Merchant Administrator during self-registration, first-time setup, merchant profile completion, branch creation, staff email invitation, account configuration, authentication, support requests, subscription activity, payment or billing activity, report access, and platform use.\n\n11.2. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data from Customers, Merchant Administrators, Merchant Branch account users, Merchant Human Resource account users, Merchant Finance account users, Merchant Front Office account users, Merchant Personnel account users, Merchant Audit account users, Clients, invited users, authorised representatives, support contacts, and records generated through Servana workflows.\n\n11.3. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data automatically from browsers, devices, networks, APIs, cookies, local storage, session storage, security systems, logs, monitoring systems, analytics technologies, infrastructure systems, error tracking systems, and product telemetry.\n\n11.4. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data from integrations, email delivery services, SMS or WhatsApp services where enabled, billing processes, payment-related processes where applicable, third-party services, public sources where lawful, professional advisers, regulators, courts, law enforcement agencies, and other lawful sources.\n\n---\n\n## 12. PURPOSES OF PROCESSING\n\n12.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data for the following purposes:\n\n12.1.1. enabling Merchant Administrator self-registration, automatic merchant tenant creation, assignment of the registering user as Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator, first-time setup, branch creation, merchant profile management, and merchant dashboard access;\n\n12.1.2. enabling the Merchant Administrator to select and update the merchant service fee tier where allowed, including Customer Centric, Split Tier, and Business Centric invoice pricing behaviour;\n\n12.1.3. enabling merchant profile setup, business category setup, merchant logo upload for invoices and receipts, branch profile setup, branch operating calendars, branch status management, branch reports, and merchant-level administration;\n\n12.1.4. enabling the Merchant Administrator to add initial Merchant Branch account user email addresses and Merchant Human Resource account user email addresses, select the applicable branch of operation, trigger welcome emails, and manage permitted staff lifecycle functions;\n\n12.1.5. providing identity and access management, Magic Link authentication, one-time-use tokens, token expiry, email verification, tenant verification, role verification, account status verification, branch-scope verification where applicable, session management, login rate limiting, login audit logs, and optional MFA where enabled;\n\n12.1.6. operating Servana’s multi-tenant SaaS environment, including tenant isolation, role-based access control, permission-based access control, branch-based access control, dashboard access, APIs, reports, exports, notifications, support, billing, and audit logs;\n\n12.1.7. supporting branch operations, including branch creation, branch profile management, operating hours, calendar exceptions, public holiday exceptions, special closures, same-day emergency closures, branch status, branch service configuration, branch service pricing, branch queues, branch appointments, branch day opening and closing, cash-up and reconciliation, branch invoice and receipt numbering, branch reports, and branch audit logs;\n\n12.1.8. supporting staff, HR, and role management, including staff invitations, activation emails, staff profile creation, employment data, role assignment, branch-scoped assignment, service eligibility, availability calendars, staff roster search, roster export, suspension, deactivation, Magic Link invalidation, active session invalidation, permission previews, role history, branch history, status history, and audit logs;\n\n12.1.9. supporting client records, including client profiles, phone numbers, optional email addresses, optional gender, visit history, service history, assigned personnel history, payment history, receipt history, preferences, preferred personnel history, notes, consent records, communication preferences, and same-branch duplicate-client prevention;\n\n12.1.10. supporting appointments, walk-ins, queue management, service sessions, personnel assignment, preferred personnel selection, estimated wait time, appointment status history, notification triggers, conflict prevention, branch closure protection, queue capacity, no-show handling, queue cancellation, reassignment, and double-booking prevention;\n\n12.1.11. supporting invoices, offline payment recording, payment validation, payment references, partial payments, split payments, duplicate payment-reference detection, external refund records, payment disputes, receipts, receipt download logs, receipt reversals, receipt reissues, and financial audit records;\n\n12.1.12. supporting the Citrus Billing Engine, including platform service fee calculation, account-opening fee tracking where applicable, merchant service fee tier storage, platform fee ledgers, merchant balances, branch-level platform fee debt tracking, overdue balance tracking, suspension triggers, fee exemptions, billing audit logs, merchant-facing statements, and platform fee disputes;\n\n12.1.13. supporting commission tracking, including commission rules per personnel, role, service, branch, cumulative roles, all personnel, individual staff, fixed amounts, percentages, payment-dependent commission status, pending commission, earned commission, optional paid commission, reversals on voided invoices, adjustments on refunds, preferred personnel surcharge commission settings, commission liability review, commission disputes, and period summaries;\n\n12.1.14. protecting client data through contact access restrictions, no Merchant Personnel bulk contact export, sensitive client contact and payment data masking, unauthorised contact-access attempt logging, consent record tracking, signed URLs for permitted exports, and contact-access or legally permitted export audit logs;\n\n12.1.15. sending service communications, administrative notices, transactional messages, security messages, Magic Link login emails, staff welcome emails, staff activation emails, staff invitation emails, staff invitation resend notices, staff invitation revocation notices, appointment confirmations, appointment cancellations, queue updates, preferred personnel wait confirmations, payment validation notices, receipt availability notices, merchant suspension warnings, platform fee overdue warnings, duplicate payment reference alerts, cash-up review notices, branch cash-up discrepancy alerts, refund approval requests, invoice void approval requests, and financial-period lock notices;\n\n12.1.16. monitoring, detecting, preventing, investigating, and responding to unauthorised access, tenant isolation violations, fraud, abuse, duplicate payment references, suspicious usage, security incidents, policy violations, billing avoidance, platform misuse, and unlawful activity;\n\n12.1.17. maintaining audit logs for merchant self-registration, tenant creation, profile changes, branch creation, branch status changes, user invitations, user creation, role changes, branch access changes, staff suspension or deactivation, Magic Link login events, unauthorised access attempts, service changes, client record changes, duplicate-client prevention events, queue changes, preferred personnel selection and override, appointment changes, service session events, invoice creation, invoice adjustment, invoice void, payment recording, payment editing, payment validation, payment rejection, payment disputes, duplicate payment-reference overrides, receipt generation, receipt reissue, external refunds, commission rule changes, financial period locks, financial period reopening, cash-up review, finance export generation, contact-access/export attempts, and platform fee setting changes;\n\n12.1.18. providing customer support, troubleshooting, diagnostics, bug fixing, remote assistance where authorised, account recovery, service restoration, incident response, and operational continuity;\n\n12.1.19. performing analytics, diagnostics, product telemetry, service improvement, performance monitoring, error tracking, capacity planning, product roadmap planning, security hardening, fraud prevention, and service reliability analysis;\n\n12.1.20. complying with Applicable Law, legal obligations, tax and accounting duties, regulatory requests, court orders, subpoenas, law enforcement requests, data protection obligations, breach response obligations, evidentiary preservation duties, and audit obligations;\n\n12.1.21. enforcing terms, customer agreements, acceptable use obligations, billing obligations, platform policies, legal rights, claims, defences, remedies, and security controls;\n\n12.1.22. supporting backups, disaster recovery, business continuity, redundancy, archival copies, scheduled jobs, queue processing, background reports, imports, exports, emails, notifications, and production monitoring;\n\n12.1.23. conducting corporate transactions, including merger, acquisition, financing, restructuring, insolvency, due diligence, asset sale, transfer, or similar business events;\n\n12.1.24. sending direct marketing, product updates, surveys, relationship communications, and lawful commercial communications where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 13. LAWFUL BASES FOR PROCESSING\n\n13.1. Citrus Labs Limited relies on one or more lawful bases depending on the Processing context, jurisdiction, role, Personal Data category, and relationship between Citrus Labs Limited, the Customer, the Merchant Administrator, and the Data Subject.\n\n13.2. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for performance of a contract or to take steps relating to a contract, including merchant self-registration, account administration, subscription administration, SaaS delivery, platform operation, authentication, support, billing, reports, and Customer administration.\n\n13.3. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for legitimate interests pursued by Citrus Labs Limited, the Customer, or another lawful party, provided such interests are not overridden by the rights and freedoms of the Data Subject under Applicable Law. Legitimate interests may include platform security, tenant isolation, fraud prevention, abuse prevention, service improvement, analytics, billing enforcement, auditability, dispute resolution, customer support, operational continuity, product development, internal accountability, legal defence, and commercial administration.\n\n13.4. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where the Data Subject has given consent, including for optional communications, optional cookies, optional profile fields, optional future features, certain marketing communications, or Processing of certain Sensitive Personal Data where consent is required by Applicable Law.\n\n13.5. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for compliance with legal obligations, including data protection obligations, cybersecurity obligations, tax obligations, accounting obligations, regulatory cooperation, court process, law enforcement cooperation, breach notification, audit obligations, and legally required retention.\n\n13.6. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary to protect vital interests, respond to emergencies, prevent serious harm, or protect life or safety, where applicable.\n\n13.7. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for a task carried out in the public interest or under lawful authority, where such basis applies.\n\n13.8. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor, Citrus Labs Limited Processes Personal Data on documented Customer instructions, subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, and the relevant data processing agreement.\n\n---\n\n## 14. CONSENT\n\n14.1. Where consent is used as the lawful basis for Processing, Citrus Labs Limited may obtain, document, and manage consent through account flows, checkboxes, settings, consent banners, cookie tools, written confirmations, electronic acceptance, role-based approvals, Customer-configured consent records, or other lawful methods.\n\n14.2. Consent may be withdrawn where Applicable Law permits withdrawal. Withdrawal does not affect Processing conducted before withdrawal and does not prevent continued Processing where another lawful basis applies, including contractual necessity, legal obligation, legitimate interests, security, legal claims, retention obligations, or Customer instructions.\n\n14.3. Where a Customer is responsible for obtaining consent from its Clients, staff, personnel, contractors, employees, branch users, or other Data Subjects, the Customer and Merchant Administrator remain responsible for the validity, scope, content, recordkeeping, and lawfulness of that consent.\n\n14.4. Citrus Labs Limited may refuse to act on an attempted withdrawal of consent where Processing is required or permitted by law, required for security, required to preserve audit logs, necessary to perform a contract, necessary to comply with legal obligations, or necessary to defend legal claims.\n\n---\n\n## 15. LEGITIMATE INTERESTS\n\n15.1. Citrus Labs Limited’s legitimate interests include operating Servana as a reliable multi-tenant SaaS platform, protecting merchant tenants against cross-tenant exposure, preventing unauthorised access, preventing fraud, enforcing access controls, detecting duplicate payment references, detecting suspicious usage, preserving audit trails, providing support, improving service quality, maintaining business continuity, enforcing rights, billing Customers, managing commercial operations, and protecting Citrus Labs Limited, Customers, Users, Clients, and third parties.\n\n15.2. Citrus Labs Limited may conduct legitimate-interest assessments where required by Applicable Law and may apply safeguards, including data minimisation, role-based access, masking, logging, retention limits, opt-outs where applicable, and objection handling.\n\n---\n\n## 16. ACCOUNT REGISTRATION AND AUTHENTICATION DISCLOSURES\n\n16.1. The Merchant Administrator registers the merchant business account. The system creates the merchant tenant and assigns the registering user as Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator.\n\n16.2. Merchant Administrator login is conducted by Magic Link sent to email. A user must not log in merely because the email address exists. Servana may verify that the email exists, the user belongs to the correct merchant tenant, the account is active, the role is active, the user has not been suspended, the branch scope is valid where applicable, and the Magic Link is valid, unused, and unexpired.\n\n16.3. Citrus Labs Limited may Process login events, failed login attempts, Magic Link requests, token metadata, IP addresses, user agents, account status, suspension status, branch-scope metadata, session status, and security events.\n\n16.4. Citrus Labs Limited may rate-limit registration, login, Magic Link, invitation acceptance, password reset where applicable, API, export, and sensitive endpoints to protect Servana against brute force, credential stuffing, abuse, and unauthorised access.\n\n---\n\n## 17. CUSTOMER ORGANISATION, ADMINISTRATOR, AND TENANT ACCOUNT DISCLOSURES\n\n17.1. The Merchant Administrator acts as a Customer-side administrator with authority over the Customer’s own merchant tenant, subject to role limitations, branch debt rules, historical-record protection, audit logs, Citrus platform controls, and Applicable Law.\n\n17.2. The Merchant Administrator may access, configure, administer, view, restrict, suspend, remove, delete, export, receive reports about, or monitor certain account data within the Customer’s own tenant, depending on the specific feature, permission, debt status, retention rule, and platform configuration.\n\n17.3. The Merchant Administrator’s administrative visibility may include Client records, branch reports, staff records, branch revenue, service pricing, service revenue, personnel performance, invoices, payment methods, platform fees, commission liabilities, preferred personnel demand, daily branch day-close PDF reports, daily branch cash-up PDF reports, and operational audit records.\n\n17.4. The Merchant Administrator must ensure that all administrator actions are lawful, necessary, proportionate, authorised, and consistent with Customer policies, employment obligations, data protection obligations, and the rights of affected Data Subjects.\n\n17.5. Citrus Labs Limited may log administrator actions, including before-and-after values for sensitive changes, actor identifiers, role, branch, merchant, IP address, user agent, timestamp, severity, and event status.\n\n---\n\n## 18. USER-GENERATED CONTENT, UPLOADED DATA, AND SERVANA RECORDS\n\n18.1. Servana may Process user-generated content, uploaded content, notes, attachments, screenshots, evidence, service notes, dispute materials, refund materials, branch records, staff records, client records, merchant logos, invoices, receipts, payment records, reports, exports, and other Customer Data.\n\n18.2. The Merchant Administrator and Customer must ensure that they have lawful authority, appropriate notices, valid rights, and required consents to upload, create, transmit, store, or otherwise Process Personal Data through Servana.\n\n18.3. Citrus Labs Limited may restrict, quarantine, preserve, delete, review, or disclose uploaded content where necessary for security, support, legal compliance, abuse prevention, incident response, rights enforcement, or lawful Customer instructions.\n\n18.4. Customer Data may be visible to authorised Users within the Customer’s tenant according to role permissions, branch scopes, report configuration, export permissions, and Customer instructions.\n\n---\n\n## 19. COMMUNICATIONS, NOTIFICATIONS, AND MARKETING\n\n19.1. Citrus Labs Limited may send transactional, security, operational, administrative, support, billing, platform, and legal communications relating to Servana.\n\n19.2. Servana may send Magic Link login emails, staff welcome emails, staff activation emails, staff invitation emails, staff invitation resend notices, staff invitation revocation notices, appointment confirmations, appointment cancellation notices, queue updates, preferred personnel wait confirmations, payment validation notices, receipt availability notices, merchant suspension warnings, platform fee overdue warnings, duplicate payment reference alerts, cash-up review notices, branch cash-up discrepancy alerts, refund approval request notices, invoice void approval request notices, and financial-period ready-for-lock notices.\n\n19.3. Email is a required launch channel. SMS, WhatsApp, or other channels may be enabled later or by configuration. Where such channels are enabled, Personal Data may be Processed through relevant communication service providers.\n\n19.4. Transactional, security, legal, administrative, and service-critical communications may be sent even where a user has opted out of marketing communications, to the extent permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n19.5. Citrus Labs Limited may send marketing or product communications where lawful, subject to applicable consent, opt-out, unsubscribe, or objection rights.\n\n---\n\n## 20. COOKIES, LOCAL STORAGE, SDKs, AND TRACKING TECHNOLOGIES\n\n20.1. Servana may use cookies, pixels, SDKs, local storage, browser storage, session storage, tags, analytics technologies, security cookies, preference technologies, session cookies, device identifiers, and similar technologies.\n\n20.2. Such technologies may be used to authenticate users, maintain sessions, support Magic Link flows, remember preferences, persist theme choices, secure accounts, detect abuse, prevent fraud, measure product usage, troubleshoot errors, improve performance, analyse navigation, support analytics, and provide lawful communications.\n\n20.3. Users may control certain cookies through browser settings, consent tools, or platform preferences where available. Blocking cookies or browser storage may impair authentication, session persistence, account security, dashboard functionality, preference storage, and other Servana features.\n\n20.4. Citrus Labs Limited will apply consent, notice, or opt-out mechanisms for non-essential cookies or tracking technologies where required by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 21. ANALYTICS, DIAGNOSTICS, TELEMETRY, AND SERVICE IMPROVEMENT\n\n21.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Usage Data, Device Data, diagnostics, product telemetry, performance data, service logs, error reports, audit metadata, queue performance data, export activity, report activity, and aggregated statistics to operate, secure, improve, troubleshoot, and scale Servana.\n\n21.2. Citrus Labs Limited may use analytics to understand dashboard performance, feature adoption, system errors, slow endpoints, failed jobs, report generation, email delivery, notification reliability, queue processing, export use, user onboarding, and general service performance.\n\n21.3. Citrus Labs Limited may create aggregated, anonymised, statistical, or de-identified data from Servana records for internal analytics, product development, commercial planning, security, benchmarking, risk assessment, support prioritisation, and performance monitoring, provided such Processing complies with Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 22. AUTOMATION, RULES ENGINES, DECISION SUPPORT, AND PROFILING\n\n22.1. Servana may use automated rules, calculations, status logic, notifications, alerts, dashboards, workflow controls, and decision-support mechanisms to support platform operations.\n\n22.2. Such mechanisms may include Magic Link expiry, tenant access checks, branch access checks, duplicate-client prevention, duplicate payment-reference detection, queue status transitions, estimated wait calculations, appointment conflict prevention, branch closure protection, service eligibility checks, preferred personnel fee calculation, service fee tier invoice pricing, platform fee calculations, platform fee overdue indicators, suspension triggers, commission calculations, payment validation status logic, receipt generation locks, financial period lock logic, cash-up discrepancy alerts, suspicious usage alerts, and audit severity classifications.\n\n22.3. Servana does not, by this Policy alone, represent that it makes solely automated decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects on Data Subjects without human involvement. Where Applicable Law grants rights relating to automated decision-making or profiling, Citrus Labs Limited will comply to the extent required.\n\n22.4. Automated indicators, risk alerts, duplicate-reference alerts, overdue warnings, and suspicious usage flags are support tools and must be interpreted in context by authorised persons where human review is required by Applicable Law, contract, or internal policy.\n\n---\n\n## 23. DATA SHARING AND DISCLOSURE\n\n23.1. Citrus Labs Limited may disclose Personal Data to the following categories of recipients where lawful and necessary:\n\n23.1.1. authorised Citrus Labs Limited personnel, Super Administrators, support personnel, engineers, security personnel, finance personnel, legal personnel, auditors, contractors, and other authorised representatives subject to confidentiality and access controls;\n\n23.1.2. the Customer, Merchant Administrator, authorised merchant users, branch users, HR users, finance users, front-office users, personnel users, audit users, and invited users according to Customer configuration, tenant scope, branch scope, role permissions, and Applicable Law;\n\n23.1.3. hosting providers, cloud infrastructure providers, database providers, object storage providers, content delivery providers, monitoring providers, email delivery providers, queue service providers, analytics providers, error tracking providers, security providers, support vendors, and other technical service providers;\n\n23.1.4. payment processors, billing providers, banks, finance partners, or payment service providers where platform fee payment, subscription billing, account-opening fee tracking, statements, or other payment-related functions are enabled;\n\n23.1.5. integration partners, API recipients, webhooks, customer-selected tools, third-party applications, external services, app stores, or embedded services where enabled by the Customer or user;\n\n23.1.6. professional advisers, lawyers, auditors, accountants, consultants, insurers, risk advisers, and business advisers;\n\n23.1.7. regulators, data protection authorities, tax authorities, courts, law enforcement, public authorities, and government bodies where required or permitted by law;\n\n23.1.8. counterparties, advisers, investors, lenders, acquirers, successors, administrators, liquidators, or transferees in connection with mergers, acquisitions, financing, restructuring, insolvency, asset sales, due diligence, or similar transactions;\n\n23.1.9. third parties where disclosure is necessary to protect rights, safety, security, property, users, Customers, Citrus Labs Limited, Servana, or the public.\n\n23.2. Citrus Labs Limited does not permit one merchant to access another merchant’s tenant data except through lawful, controlled, authorised Citrus Labs Limited workflows.\n\n23.3. Citrus Labs Limited may disclose Personal Data where instructed by the Customer, configured by the Customer, necessary to provide Servana, necessary to comply with law, necessary to enforce rights, necessary to resolve disputes, or necessary to protect security and integrity.\n\n---\n\n## 24. NO SALE OF PERSONAL DATA\n\n24.1. Citrus Labs Limited does not sell Personal Data in the ordinary sense of exchanging identifiable Personal Data for monetary consideration as a data broker.\n\n24.2. This clause does not restrict Citrus Labs Limited from using service providers, appointing Subprocessors, conducting analytics, creating aggregated or de-identified information, disclosing Personal Data in a corporate transaction, providing Servana, processing subscriptions, supporting Customers, enforcing agreements, complying with law, securing the platform, or making other lawful disclosures permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 25. SUBPROCESSORS AND SERVICE PROVIDERS\n\n25.1. Citrus Labs Limited may appoint Subprocessors and service providers to support hosting, storage, authentication, email delivery, notifications, analytics, monitoring, security, backups, support, billing, reporting, exports, infrastructure, development, deployment, maintenance, and other operational functions.\n\n25.2. Citrus Labs Limited will use reasonable contractual, organisational, and technical safeguards with service providers and Subprocessors, including confidentiality obligations, access restrictions, security obligations, lawful Processing limitations, and appropriate contractual protections where required by Applicable Law.\n\n25.3. Citrus Labs Limited may change Subprocessors and service providers where necessary for operational, technical, legal, security, commercial, availability, or business continuity reasons, subject to any notice, objection, or approval rights expressly required by an applicable data processing agreement or Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 26. CROSS-BORDER TRANSFERS\n\n26.1. Personal Data may be stored, hosted, accessed, supported, backed up, transmitted, or otherwise Processed in Kenya, elsewhere in Africa, or outside Africa depending on hosting, infrastructure, support, vendor, backup, security, corporate, and technical requirements.\n\n26.2. Cross-border transfers may occur where Citrus Labs Limited uses cloud hosting, object storage, email providers, support tools, monitoring tools, analytics tools, security services, communication providers, professional advisers, affiliates, contractors, or other vendors located outside the jurisdiction where the Data Subject resides.\n\n26.3. Citrus Labs Limited will implement appropriate safeguards for cross-border transfers where required by Applicable Law, which may include contractual safeguards, data processing agreements, transfer assessments, consent where valid, regulatory approvals where required, or other lawful transfer mechanisms.\n\n26.4. Where a Customer requires specific data localisation, data residency, or jurisdiction-specific storage arrangements, such requirements must be expressly agreed in writing. Citrus Labs Limited does not guarantee jurisdiction-specific storage unless expressly agreed or required by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 27. DATA LOCALISATION AND JURISDICTION-SPECIFIC STORAGE REQUIREMENTS\n\n27.1. Certain jurisdictions may impose sector-specific, category-specific, or regulator-specific storage, localisation, access, transfer, or recordkeeping requirements.\n\n27.2. Citrus Labs Limited will comply with applicable data localisation or storage requirements to the extent such requirements apply to Citrus Labs Limited, Servana, the relevant Customer, the relevant Data Subject, or the relevant Processing activity.\n\n27.3. The Customer must inform Citrus Labs Limited in writing where the Customer’s use case is subject to special storage, localisation, sectoral, or jurisdiction-specific requirements not reasonably apparent from ordinary use of Servana.\n\n---\n\n## 28. DATA RETENTION\n\n28.1. Citrus Labs Limited retains Personal Data for as long as reasonably necessary to provide Servana, administer accounts, support Customers, secure the platform, maintain audit trails, comply with law, enforce rights, resolve disputes, preserve evidence, satisfy tax or accounting obligations, manage backups, maintain business continuity, and fulfil the purposes described in this Policy.\n\n28.2. Retention periods may differ depending on data type, role, Customer configuration, account status, subscription status, payment status, branch status, dispute status, legal requirements, operational necessity, backup cycles, and contractual obligations.\n\n28.3. Merchant Administrator accounts may be suspended after three months of consecutive no usage and may be deleted after six months of consecutive no usage, inclusive of related merchant branch accounts and respective merchant staff accounts, where the applicable inactivity rule is triggered. Consecutive no usage refers to the Merchant Administrator not making a platform fee payment within the relevant period, subject to applicable platform terms, lawful exceptions, and operational implementation.\n\n28.4. Historical records, financial records, receipts, invoices, payment records, platform fee records, branch records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission records, dispute records, audit logs, and legally necessary accounting records may be preserved or archived and must not be silently destroyed merely because an account is inactive, suspended, deleted, or terminated.\n\n28.5. Audit logs may be retained for extended periods because they are necessary for security, accountability, dispute resolution, compliance, legal claims, tenant isolation, and evidentiary integrity.\n\n28.6. Backups, disaster recovery copies, archival records, immutable logs, and technical residual copies may persist for limited periods after deletion from active systems and may be deleted according to backup rotation, legal hold, archival, or disaster recovery procedures.\n\n28.7. Citrus Labs Limited may retain anonymised, aggregated, or de-identified data indefinitely where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 29. DELETION, ACCOUNT CLOSURE, SUSPENSION, TERMINATION, AND POST-TERMINATION HANDLING\n\n29.1. Citrus Labs Limited may suspend, restrict, deactivate, or terminate accounts where permitted or required by customer agreements, Applicable Law, security requirements, billing enforcement, abuse controls, inactivity rules, platform-policy enforcement, or internal authorisation controls.\n\n29.2. Merchant Branch accounts may be suspended because of billing issues, abuse, platform-policy enforcement, or Merchant Administrator action. Historical records may remain visible, preserved, archived, or restricted as applicable.\n\n29.3. A branch must not be closed or archived while live operational records exist, including active queue entries, in-progress service sessions, unpaid invoices, pending payment validations, unissued receipts for validated payments, pending appointment check-ins, unclosed branch days, or unresolved cash-up discrepancies.\n\n29.4. Suspension or deactivation of merchant staff users may invalidate active sessions, invalidate unused Magic Links, block login, preserve historical operational records, and trigger reassignment checks for live queues, appointments, and service sessions.\n\n29.5. Termination of a Customer account may result in restriction of active access, export opportunities where contractually available, deletion or archival of Customer Data, continued retention where legally or operationally necessary, and preservation of audit logs, billing records, security logs, and dispute records.\n\n29.6. Deletion requests are subject to lawful retention, audit preservation, security, legal claims, tax, accounting, backup, and Customer-controlled data limitations.\n\n---\n\n## 30. SECURITY MEASURES\n\n30.1. Citrus Labs Limited uses administrative, technical, organisational, and physical safeguards designed to protect Personal Data against unauthorised access, loss, misuse, alteration, disclosure, destruction, or compromise.\n\n30.2. Safeguards may include secure authentication, Magic Link controls, one-time-use tokens, token expiry, session timeout, rate limiting, role-based access control, permission-based access control, tenant-based access control, branch-based access control, access logging, security monitoring, audit logs, least privilege, optional MFA for high-privilege roles, signed URLs for private downloads, encryption where appropriate, HTTPS in production, CSRF protection, strict CORS rules, secure cookie configuration, validation of incoming requests, file upload controls, private file storage, secrets management, dependency vulnerability scanning, backups, monitoring, alerting, and incident response procedures.\n\n30.3. Servana’s technical design may include server-side authorisation, Laravel-supported authentication mechanisms, Laravel Policies or Gates, permission packages, validation controls, guarded or fillable model rules, database indexes, tenant ownership keys, public-safe identifiers, database transactions, pagination, background queues, structured logs, performance monitoring, failed job tracking, queue monitoring, production health checks, and vulnerability scanning.\n\n30.4. Frontend permission checks may improve user experience but must not be treated as security controls. Backend, API, form submission, export, download, billing, account settings, invitation, removal, and admin screens must be authorised server-side.\n\n30.5. No electronic system, internet transmission, cloud environment, authentication process, storage mechanism, or SaaS platform can be guaranteed absolutely secure. Citrus Labs Limited does not warrant that unauthorised access, cyberattack, data loss, misuse, or security incident can never occur.\n\n---\n\n## 31. MERCHANT ADMINISTRATOR RESPONSIBILITIES\n\n31.1. The Merchant Administrator is responsible for protecting Magic Links, email accounts, devices, sessions, authentication factors, account access, and administrative permissions.\n\n31.2. The Merchant Administrator must ensure that data submitted to Servana is accurate, lawful, necessary, proportionate, and authorised.\n\n31.3. The Merchant Administrator must ensure that the Customer has lawful authority to Process Personal Data relating to Clients, staff, personnel, employees, contractors, branch users, finance users, audit users, service recipients, and other Data Subjects.\n\n31.4. The Merchant Administrator must not create false records, manipulate invoices, submit fake payment references, suppress disputes, misuse client contact details, export data without authority, bypass access controls, share Magic Links, assign roles unlawfully, or use Servana in violation of Applicable Law.\n\n31.5. The Merchant Administrator must promptly notify Citrus Labs Limited of suspected unauthorised access, account compromise, mistaken disclosure, inaccurate data, platform misuse, security vulnerability, unlawful Processing, or any incident that may affect Personal Data.\n\n---\n\n## 32. CUSTOMER RESPONSIBILITIES\n\n32.1. The Customer is responsible for determining the lawfulness of its Processing activities in Servana, including lawful basis, notices, consents, employee and contractor disclosures, client disclosures, branch policies, staff permissions, service-recipient communications, payment recordkeeping, export controls, role assignments, and retention requirements.\n\n32.2. The Customer must provide appropriate privacy notices to its Clients, staff, personnel, employees, contractors, branch users, finance users, front-office users, audit users, and other Data Subjects where required by Applicable Law.\n\n32.3. The Customer is responsible for handling Data Subject requests relating to Customer-controlled data, unless Applicable Law or a written agreement requires otherwise.\n\n32.4. The Customer must configure Servana in a lawful manner, including role-based access, branch scope, finance permissions, audit visibility, contact access, export restrictions, staff availability, service eligibility, and notification settings.\n\n32.5. The Customer must not configure or use Servana to unlawfully expose Personal Data, bypass role restrictions, grant excessive permissions, export client contact data without lawful authority, record inaccurate payment references knowingly, suppress consent records, delete required financial records, or violate Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 33. DATA SUBJECT RIGHTS\n\n33.1. Subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, verification requirements, Customer-control limitations, and contractual obligations, Data Subjects may have rights to request access, correction, rectification, deletion, erasure, restriction, objection, portability, withdrawal of consent, objection to direct marketing, review of certain automated decisions, or other rights available under Applicable Law.\n\n33.2. Rights are not absolute. Citrus Labs Limited may refuse, limit, delay, or condition a request where permitted or required by law, including where necessary for security, fraud prevention, legal claims, evidentiary preservation, tax or accounting obligations, audit logs, contractual performance, Customer instructions, competing rights, excessive requests, manifestly unfounded requests, or legal prohibitions.\n\n33.3. Where a request relates to Customer-controlled data, Citrus Labs Limited may direct the Data Subject to the relevant Customer or Merchant Administrator, assist the Customer in responding, or respond directly where permitted or required by Applicable Law.\n\n33.4. Requests may be submitted to [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke). Citrus Labs Limited may require identity verification, authority verification for agents, account confirmation, request clarification, and information sufficient to locate the relevant data.\n\n---\n\n## 34. PROCEDURE FOR EXERCISING RIGHTS\n\n34.1. A request should identify the Data Subject, the relevant account, merchant tenant, branch, role, record type, requested right, and sufficient information to enable Citrus Labs Limited to assess and respond.\n\n34.2. Citrus Labs Limited may verify identity through account credentials, email verification, support verification, documentation, authorised-agent confirmation, or other reasonable methods.\n\n34.3. Citrus Labs Limited will respond within timelines required by Applicable Law, subject to lawful extensions, request complexity, request volume, Customer involvement, verification delays, legal restrictions, and technical feasibility.\n\n34.4. Citrus Labs Limited may charge a lawful fee or refuse repetitive, excessive, abusive, unfounded, or unlawful requests where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 35. REQUESTS WHERE CITRUS LABS LIMITED ACTS AS PROCESSOR\n\n35.1. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor for Customer-controlled Personal Data, Citrus Labs Limited may refer Data Subject requests to the Customer, notify the Customer, or assist the Customer in fulfilling the request, subject to the applicable data processing agreement and Applicable Law.\n\n35.2. Citrus Labs Limited may not be able to fulfil a request directly where the Customer determines the relevant Processing purpose and means, where Citrus Labs Limited lacks sufficient authority, where direct fulfilment would conflict with Customer instructions, or where direct fulfilment would impair another Data Subject’s rights or legal obligations.\n\n35.3. The Customer and Merchant Administrator must cooperate with Citrus Labs Limited where necessary to respond to Data Subject requests, regulatory requests, security incidents, legal claims, or lawful investigations involving Customer-controlled data.\n\n---\n\n## 36. COMPLAINTS AND REGULATORY CONTACTS\n\n36.1. Privacy complaints, questions, or requests may be directed to Citrus Labs Limited at [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke).\n\n36.2. Where Applicable Law provides a right to complain to a competent data protection authority, a Data Subject may lodge a complaint with the relevant authority. In Kenya, the competent authority may include the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner where applicable.\n\n36.3. Citrus Labs Limited may cooperate with competent authorities to the extent required by Applicable Law and may require Customers and Merchant Administrators to cooperate where the complaint concerns Customer-controlled data.\n\n---\n\n## 37. DATA BREACHES AND SECURITY INCIDENTS\n\n37.1. Citrus Labs Limited maintains procedures to identify, investigate, mitigate, document, and respond to security incidents and Personal Data breaches.\n\n37.2. Where Citrus Labs Limited determines that notification is required by Applicable Law, Citrus Labs Limited will notify affected Customers, Data Subjects, regulators, or other parties as legally required, taking into account the nature of the incident, Personal Data involved, likely consequences, mitigation measures, legal instructions, and ongoing investigations.\n\n37.3. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor, Citrus Labs Limited will notify the relevant Customer of a Personal Data breach in accordance with the applicable data processing agreement and Applicable Law.\n\n37.4. The Merchant Administrator must cooperate with Citrus Labs Limited in incident investigation, containment, mitigation, communication, evidence preservation, credential resets, access review, and corrective action where the incident relates to the Customer’s tenant, users, devices, credentials, configurations, or Customer-controlled data.\n\n37.5. Any notification, cooperation, investigation, mitigation, or remedial step shall not be construed as an admission of fault, liability, causation, negligence, unlawfulness, or damages.\n\n---\n\n## 38. LEGAL PROCESS, LAW ENFORCEMENT, AND REGULATORY REQUESTS\n\n38.1. Citrus Labs Limited may disclose Personal Data where required or permitted by Applicable Law, court order, subpoena, warrant, regulatory demand, law enforcement request, public authority request, tax authority request, national security request, fraud investigation, emergency disclosure, legal claim, or rights enforcement.\n\n38.2. Citrus Labs Limited may challenge, narrow, delay, or refuse requests where legally permissible and appropriate.\n\n38.3. Citrus Labs Limited may notify affected Customers or Data Subjects of legal requests where permitted, unless prohibited by law, court order, security necessity, investigation requirements, or risk of harm.\n\n38.4. Citrus Labs Limited may preserve Personal Data where necessary to comply with legal process, regulatory inquiry, litigation hold, dispute resolution, security investigation, billing dispute, or enforcement of rights.\n\n---\n\n## 39. BUSINESS TRANSFERS\n\n39.1. Personal Data may be disclosed, transferred, assigned, or otherwise Processed in connection with a merger, acquisition, financing, restructuring, insolvency, sale of assets, transfer of business, change of control, due diligence review, investment transaction, or similar event.\n\n39.2. Any recipient of Personal Data in such context shall be subject to appropriate confidentiality, security, and lawful Processing obligations to the extent required by Applicable Law and transaction circumstances.\n\n---\n\n## 40. THIRD-PARTY WEBSITES, INTEGRATIONS, APIs, AND EXTERNAL SERVICES\n\n40.1. Servana may contain links to third-party websites, external services, app stores, embedded services, integration partners, APIs, communication channels, payment or billing services, or Customer-selected tools.\n\n40.2. Citrus Labs Limited is not responsible for the privacy practices, security, content, terms, or Processing activities of third-party services not controlled by Citrus Labs Limited.\n\n40.3. Where a Customer enables an integration, export, webhook, API connection, external communication channel, or third-party tool, the Customer and Merchant Administrator are responsible for ensuring that such integration is lawful, properly configured, secure, and consistent with obligations to Data Subjects.\n\n---\n\n## 41. OPEN-SOURCE, THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE, AND INFRASTRUCTURE DEPENDENCIES\n\n41.1. Servana may use open-source software, commercial software, frameworks, libraries, infrastructure components, cloud services, queues, cache systems, databases, object storage, search systems, monitoring tools, and development dependencies.\n\n41.2. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data through such dependencies only to the extent necessary for Servana’s operation, security, maintenance, hosting, storage, monitoring, support, deployment, and development.\n\n41.3. Citrus Labs Limited does not disclose confidential internal architecture beyond what is necessary in this Policy and applicable agreements.\n\n---\n\n## 42. PAYMENT AND BILLING DATA\n\n42.1. Servana records offline merchant-client payment information. Supported offline payment methods may include cash, M-Pesa, bank transfer, card terminal, voucher, split payment, and other merchant-defined offline payment methods.\n\n42.2. Servana may record payment method, payment amount, payment reference, payment date and time, payment note, recorded-by user, validated-by user, validation status, validation note, rejection reason, branch identifier, merchant identifier, invoice identifier, payment record identifier, receipt identifier, duplicate reference status, dispute status, external refund status, and cash-up reconciliation data.\n\n42.3. Servana does not, by this Policy alone, directly process client-to-merchant payments inside the platform. Where payment processing is enabled through a third-party payment processor or future feature, additional payment terms, processor notices, and lawful disclosures may apply.\n\n42.4. Citrus Labs Limited may Process platform fee records, subscription records, merchant balance records, account-opening fee records where applicable, service fee tier settings, billing cycle records, overdue status records, suspension triggers, fee exemption records, platform fee disputes, merchant-facing statements, and related ledgers.\n\n42.5. The Merchant Administrator acknowledges that the selected merchant service fee tier affects merchant-client invoice pricing only and does not reduce the merchant’s platform fee liability to Citrus Labs Limited unless expressly stated in applicable billing terms.\n\n---\n\n## 43. SUPPORT, TROUBLESHOOTING, DIAGNOSTICS, AND REMOTE ASSISTANCE\n\n43.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data in support interactions, troubleshooting, diagnostics, bug reports, screenshots, attachments, logs, API traces, export issues, invoice issues, queue issues, payment validation issues, receipt issues, audit issues, account access issues, and other support matters.\n\n43.2. Support access may be restricted, logged, audited, time-bound, and limited to the information reasonably necessary to resolve the support matter, investigate abuse, secure the platform, comply with law, or enforce rights.\n\n43.3. Users must not submit unnecessary Sensitive Personal Data in support requests, attachments, screenshots, or diagnostic materials.\n\n---\n\n## 44. BACKUPS, DISASTER RECOVERY, AND BUSINESS CONTINUITY\n\n44.1. Citrus Labs Limited may create and maintain backups, disaster recovery copies, redundancy systems, archival records, restoration points, logs, and business continuity procedures.\n\n44.2. Backup data may include Personal Data and may persist after deletion from active systems until the relevant backup cycle expires or the data is overwritten, unless legal hold, security investigation, regulatory obligations, or dispute preservation require longer retention.\n\n44.3. Restored data may reintroduce Personal Data into active systems where necessary for disaster recovery, incident response, legal preservation, business continuity, or service restoration.\n\n---\n\n## 45. AGGREGATED, ANONYMISED, AND DE-IDENTIFIED DATA\n\n45.1. Citrus Labs Limited may use aggregated, anonymised, or de-identified data for analytics, reporting, product improvement, security, benchmarking, commercial planning, research, capacity planning, and platform performance assessment.\n\n45.2. Citrus Labs Limited may retain and use such data after account closure, termination, deletion, expiry, or discontinuance of access to Servana, provided such use does not unlawfully identify a Data Subject.\n\n---\n\n## 46. INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY AND DATA OWNERSHIP BOUNDARIES\n\n46.1. Privacy rights are distinct from intellectual property rights, contractual rights, database rights, platform ownership, software rights, analytics rights, and commercial rights.\n\n46.2. Customers and Data Subjects may retain rights in their Personal Data as provided by Applicable Law and applicable agreements. Citrus Labs Limited retains ownership of Servana, its software, interfaces, configurations, workflows, templates, documentation, system designs, platform metadata, aggregated data, de-identified insights, security telemetry, audit structures, improvements, and intellectual property, subject to applicable agreements and law.\n\n46.3. Nothing in this Policy transfers ownership of Servana or Citrus Labs Limited intellectual property to any Merchant Administrator, Customer, User, Client, or Data Subject.\n\n---\n\n## 47. CONFIDENTIALITY AND PROTECTED BUSINESS INFORMATION\n\n47.1. Servana may Process confidential Customer business information, financial records, staff records, branch performance records, platform fee records, pricing records, service catalogues, business operations, reports, disputes, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission records, and audit logs.\n\n47.2. Citrus Labs Limited applies confidentiality obligations and access controls to protect confidential information, subject to lawful disclosure, Customer configuration, support access, legal process, professional adviser access, security needs, and applicable agreements.\n\n47.3. The Merchant Administrator must treat client records, staff records, branch records, financial records, payment records, platform records, security records, and audit records as confidential and must not disclose them except as authorised by the Customer, Citrus Labs Limited, Applicable Law, or relevant agreements.\n\n---\n\n## 48. DIRECT MARKETING AND OPT-OUT RIGHTS\n\n48.1. Citrus Labs Limited may send lawful marketing, product updates, surveys, event notices, service announcements, or commercial communications to business contacts where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n48.2. Recipients may opt out of marketing communications through available unsubscribe mechanisms or by contacting [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke).\n\n48.3. Opting out of marketing does not prevent service, legal, security, billing, transactional, or administrative communications.\n\n---\n\n## 49. CHANGES TO THIS POLICY\n\n49.1. Citrus Labs Limited may amend, update, replace, or revise this Policy from time to time to reflect changes in Servana, legal requirements, operational practices, security measures, products, features, vendors, integrations, business structure, or regulatory expectations.\n\n49.2. Citrus Labs Limited may provide notice of material changes through the website, dashboard, account portal, email, in-product notice, legal page, customer communication, or other reasonable method.\n\n49.3. Continued access to or use of Servana after the effective date of an updated Policy constitutes acknowledgement of the updated Policy, subject to Applicable Law and any rights expressly available to the Data Subject.\n\n49.4. Where Applicable Law requires consent for a specific new Processing activity, Citrus Labs Limited will seek such consent where required.\n\n---\n\n## 50. GOVERNING LAW AND INTERPRETIVE PROVISIONS\n\n50.1. This Policy is primarily anchored in Kenya because Citrus Labs Limited is located in Nairobi, Kenya, and Servana is owned and operated by Citrus Labs Limited.\n\n50.2. This Policy shall be interpreted consistently with Applicable Law, including applicable privacy and data protection laws in jurisdictions where Servana lawfully operates or where Data Subjects are protected.\n\n50.3. Nothing in this Policy limits non-waivable rights available to Data Subjects under Applicable Law.\n\n50.4. This Policy does not create warranties, service levels, payment obligations, acceptable use rules, or liability limitations except to the extent privacy-related clauses necessarily cross-reference those matters. Such matters are governed by separate applicable terms and agreements.\n\n---\n\n## 51. CONTACT INFORMATION\n\n51.1. Privacy inquiries, Data Subject requests, complaints, security concerns, and data protection communications may be directed to:\n\n**Citrus Labs Limited**\n**P.O. Box 23983 - 00100, Nairobi, Kenya**\n**Email:** [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke)\n\n51.2. Requests should include sufficient information to identify the requester, relevant account, relevant merchant tenant, relevant branch, relevant role, requested action, and affected Personal Data.\n\n---\n\n## 52. FINAL LEGAL CLAUSES\n\n52.1. If any provision of this Policy is held to be invalid, unlawful, or unenforceable, the remaining provisions shall remain valid and enforceable to the maximum extent permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n52.2. Provisions relating to retention, audit logs, security, confidentiality, legal claims, regulatory cooperation, business transfers, aggregated data, de-identified data, intellectual property, interpretation, and evidentiary effect shall survive account closure, termination, suspension, deletion, expiry, or discontinuance of access to Servana.\n\n52.3. Failure by Citrus Labs Limited to enforce any provision of this Policy shall not constitute a waiver of that provision or any other right.\n\n52.4. This Policy may be published electronically, accepted electronically, incorporated by reference, displayed in-product, linked through account flows, and retained as an electronic record. Electronic versions, logs, timestamps, acceptance records, publication records, account records, and audit records may be used as evidence to the fullest extent permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n52.5. The English-language version of this Policy governs unless Citrus Labs Limited expressly publishes a legally controlling translated version. Translations, where provided, are for convenience unless expressly stated otherwise.\n\n52.6. Headings are for convenience only and do not limit the scope or interpretation of any clause.\n\n52.7. In the event of conflict between this Policy and a more specific signed agreement governing the same Processing activity, the more specific agreement shall control for that Processing activity, unless Applicable Law requires otherwise.\n";

